== Husbands and the Love of Christ

=== §. 26. #emph[Of the summe of Husbands duties.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[EPHES. 5. 25.]

    #emph[25. #emph[Husbands loue your Wiues, euen as Christ also loued the Church, and gaue himselfe for it.]]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

FRom Wiues duties the Apostle proceedeth to presse Husbands duties. And as he propounded to Wiues for a patterne, the example of the Church, so to Husbands he propoundeth the example of Christ: and #footnote[#emph[Uers.] 28, 29.] addeth thereunto the patterne of a mans selfe, in regard of that naturall affection which he beareth to his body. Thus he addeth patterne to patterne, #footnote[Husbands duties most pressed.] and doth the more largely and earnestly presse them, because husbands hauing a more honourable place, their failing in duty is the more hainous, scandalous, and dangerous.

The Apostle restraineth the duties of Husbands to their #emph[owne] Wiues, as he did the duties of Wiues to their #emph[owne] Husbands. For though the same word be not here vsed which was before, yet a word of like emphasis is vsed: and as good reason there is that our English translators should haue put in this particle #emph[(owne)] in this verse, as in the 22. verse, for proofe whereof read 1 Cor. 7. 2. Where #footnote[See §. 82.] these two words are vsed, and both of them translated #emph[owne.]

This I haue the rather noted, because many who hold that a Wife must haue but one Husband, conceit that a Husband may haue more Wiues then one: which conceit this particle #emph[(owne)] wipeth away. All the duties of an Husband are comprised vnder this one word #footnote[See the reasons hereof #emph[Treat.] 4. §. 2.] #emph[Loue.] Wherein that an Husband might be the better directed, and whereto that he might be the rather prouoked, the forenamed example of Christ, and of his loue to the Church, is very liuely set forth: first generally in these words, #emph[euen as Christ loued the Church:] and then more particularly in the words following.

=== §. 27. #emph[Of the example of Christs loue.]

The note of comparison ( #emph[Euen as)] requireth no #emph[equality,] as if it were possible for an Husband in that #emph[measure] to loue his wife, as Christ loued his Church; (for as Christ in excellency and greatnesse exceedeth man, so in loue and tendernesse) But it noteth an #emph[equity,] and #emph[like quality.]

An #emph[equitie,] because there is as great reason that Husbands by vertue of their place should loue their Wiues, as that Christ by vertue of his place should loue the Church.

A #emph[like quality,] because the loue which Christ beareth to the Church is euery way without exception: and a loue which turneth to the good and benefit of the Church. Hence note two points.

1. #emph[Husbands must come as neare as they can to Christ in louing their wiues.] In which respect, because they can neuer loue so much as Christ did, they must neuer thinke they haue loued enough.

2. Though their loue in #emph[measure] cannot equall Christs loue, yet in the #emph[manner] thereof it must be like Christs, a #emph[preuenting, true, free, pure, exceeding, constant loue.]

#emph[The] measure #emph[and] manner #emph[of Christs loue is distinctly noted,] Treat. 4. §. 61. #emph[&c. and the loue which an husband oweth his wife paralleld & applied therto, which application may be also made of that Christian mutuall loue which we owe one to another.]

The loue of Christ to the Church is amplified,

- 1\. By an #emph[Effect] thereof, in these words, #emph[He gaue himselfe for it.]
- 2\. By the #emph[End] of that effect, largely set downe, #emph[verse] 26, 27.

The #emph[effect] is noted partly as a

+ Confirmation of the truth
+ Declaration of the measure

of Christs loue.

The #emph[Act (he gaue)] sheweth that his loue was in deed and truth: not only in shew and pretence.

The #emph[Object (himselfe)] sheweth that he loued his Church more then his owne life. A greater euidence of loue could not be giuen: for #emph[greater loue hath no man then this, that a man] #footnote[#emph[Iohn] 15. 13.] #emph[lay downe his life for his friend.]

The #emph[end] of Christs loue (set forth #emph[vers.] 26, 27.) is noted to shew that he so loued his Church for her good and happinesse, rather then for any aduantage to himselfe.

As this example of Christs loue to his Church is set before husbands: so it may and ought also to be applied to all Christians: and that in a double respect.

1. As a motiue to stirre them vp to loue both Christ himselfe, and also their brethren.

2. As a patterne to teach them how to loue.

A motiue it is to loue Christ, because loue deserueth loue: especially such a loue, of such a person as the loue of Christ is. Yea, our loue of Christ is an euidence that we are loued of Christ, as smoake is a signe of fire. Wherefore both in thankfulnesse to Christ, for his loue to vs, and for assurance to our owne soules of Christs loue to vs, we ought in all things that we can to testifie our loue to Christ.

A motiue it is also to loue our brethren, because Christ being in heauen, our #footnote[#emph[Psal.] 16. 2.] #emph[goodnesse extendeth not to him:] but our brethren on earth stand in his stead, and the loue we shew to them, we shew to him; and he accepteth it as done to him: #footnote[#emph[Matth.] 25. 35, 40.] #emph[Ye fed me, yee visited me,] saith Christ to them that fed and visited his brethren. This loue also, euen the #footnote[1 #emph[Joh.] 4. 20.] loue of our brethren, is an euidence that we are loued of God. Wherefore #footnote[#emph[& vers.] 11.] #emph[if Christ so loued vs, we ought also to loue one another.]

How the loue of Christ is a patterne, I will #footnote[Treat 4 §. 61. #emph[&c.]] afterwards shew.

=== §. 28. #emph[Of Christs giuing himselfe.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[EPHES. 5. 25.]

    #emph[And gaue himselfe for it.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

THis fruit and effect of Christs loue extendeth it selfe to all #footnote[Christ himselfe, and all that he did and suffered, are ours.] the things that Christ did or suffered for our redemption: as, that he descended from heauen, tooke vpon him our nature, and became a man; that he subiected himselfe to the law, and perfectly fulfilled it; that he made himselfe subiect to many temptations of the deuill and his instruments; that he tooke vpon him our infirmities; that he became a King to gouerne vs, a Prophet to instruct vs, a Priest to make an attonement for vs: that he subiected himselfe to death, the cursed death of the crosse, and so made himselfe an oblation & sacrifice for our sins; that he was buried; that he rose againe; that he ascended into heauen, and there sitteth at Gods right hand to make intercession for vs. For after that Christ had taken vpon him to be our #emph[head] and #emph[Sauiour,] he wholly set himselfe apart for our vse, and our benefit: so as his person, his offices, his actions, his sufferings, his humiliation, his exaltation, the dignitie, the puritie, the efficacie of all is the Churches, and to her good doe they all tend. This in generall is the extent of this fruit of Christs loue, #emph[he gaue himselfe for it.]

More particularly, we may note these three points:

- 1\. The #emph[action,] what he did, #emph[(he gaue.)]
- 2\. The #emph[obiect,] what he gaue, #emph[(himselfe.)]
- 3\. The #emph[end,] why he gaue himselfe, #emph[(for it)] for the Churches good.

The #emph[action] hauing relation to the #emph[obiect,] most especially pointeth at the death of Christ. The Greeke word is a compound word, and signifieth to #emph[giue vp.] It implieth two things,

1. #emph[That Christ willingly died:] the simple word #emph[(gaue)] intimateth so much.

2. #emph[That his death was an oblation:] that is, a price of redemption, or a satisfaction: the compound word #emph[(gaue vp)] intimateth so much.

=== §. 29. #emph[Of the willingnesse of Christ to die.]

That Christ willingly died is euident by the circumstances noted about his death: when #emph[Peter] counselled him to spare himselfe, and not to goe to Ierusalem (where he was to be put to death) #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 16. 32, 23] he called him Satan, and said, #emph[he was an offence to him:] when #emph[Iudas] went out to betray him, #footnote[#emph[Iohn] 13. 27.] he said vnto him, #emph[That thou doest, doe quickly:] #footnote[#emph[&] 18. 2. #emph[&c. Audiui, Domine, auditum tuun et expani, dum quaeris à quaerentibus quid quaerant, cùm noueris quod te quaerant: & dixisti te esse lesum Nazarenun. Cypr. de Pass. Christi.]] When #emph[Iudas] was gone out to get companie to apprehend him, he went to the place where he was wont, so as #emph[Iudas] might readily finde him; yea, he met them in the mid-way that came to take him; and he asked them whom they sought, though he knew whom they sought: and when they said, #emph[Iesus of Nazaret,] he answered, #emph[I am he:] When they came to him, he droue them all backward with a word of his mouth, and yet would not escape from them: #footnote[#emph[Matth.] 26. 53.] He could haue praied to the Father to haue had more then twelue legions of Angels for his safeguard against those that apprehended him, but would not: #footnote[#emph[&] 27. 42.] when by his aduersaries he was prouoked to haue come downe from the Crosse, and could haue done so, he would not. #footnote[#emph[Mark.] 15. 39.] At the instant of giuing vp the ghost, he cried with a loud voice: which sheweth that his life was not then spent, he might haue retained it longer if he would: and thereupon the Centurion gathered that he was the Sonne of God. #footnote[#emph[Iohn] 2. 19. #emph[Matth.] 28. 6.] When he was actually dead, and laid in a graue, he rose againe. These & other like circumstances verifie that which Christ said of himselfe, #footnote[#emph[Iohn] 10. 18. #emph[Indicia dedit nullase necessita e, sed obedientia vrgeri ad mortem. Cypr. de coen. Dom. Reason.]] #emph[No man taketh my life from me, but I lay it downe of my selfe.] It was therefore no necessitie that compelled him to die, but his voluntarie obedience.

Christ is #footnote[#emph[Acts] 3. 15..] #emph[the Lord, Prince] and #emph[Author of life,] and hath an absolute power as ouer the life of others, so ouer his owne life.

Thus then we see that his sacrifice was a voluntarie and free gift: the cause thereof was his owne will and good pleasure.

Exceedingly doth this commend the loue of Christ: and assureth vs that it is the more acceptable to God, who #emph[loueth a cheerefull giuer.] #footnote[1\. #emph[Vse.]]

Let vs in imitation of our head, doe the things whereunto #footnote[2 #emph[Cor.] 9. 7.] we are called willingly and cheerefully, though they seeme neuer #footnote[2\. #emph[Vse.]] so disgracefull to the world, or grieuous to our weake flesh.

=== §. 30. #emph[Of the kinde of Christs death, an oblation.]

That #emph[Christs death was an oblation,] and a price of redemption, is euident by the death of those beasts which were offered vp for a sacrifice, and therein were a type of Christs death. But expresly is this noted by this Apostle, where he saith, #footnote[#emph[Ephes.] 5. 2.] #emph[Christ hath giuen himselfe for vs, an offering and sacrifice to God for a sweet smelling sauour:] and againe, #footnote[1 #emph[Tim.] 2. 6.] #emph[Christ gaue himselfe a ransome.] The phrases of #footnote[1 #emph[Pet.] 1. 18, 19] #emph[redeeming,] #footnote[#emph[Acts] 20. 28.] #emph[purchaesing,] #footnote[2 #emph[Pet.] 2. 1.] #emph[buying,] with the like, attributed to Christ and his bloud, doe further confirme the same.

Learne hereby to consider Christs death, not as the death of #footnote[Vse.] a priuate man, but of a publike person, of a suertie, of a pledge, that in our roome and stead #footnote[2 #emph[Cor.] 5. 21.] #emph[was made sinne, and] #footnote[#emph[Gal.] 3. 13.] #emph[was made a curse] to redeeme vs from our sinnes, and from the curse which by sinne was fallen vpon vs. The comfort and benefit of Christs death is lost, if this be not knowne and beleeued. In this consisteth a maine difference betwixt the death of Christ, and all other men, not the most righteous Martyrs excepted. Their death was but a dutie, and debt: no satisfactory oblation, no price, no ransome, as Christs was.

=== §. 31. #emph[Of the infinite valew of the prince of our redemption.]

The Obiect, or thing which Christ gaue for a ransome was #emph[himselfe,] not his body alone, nor his body and soule only, but his person consisting of his two natures, humane, and diuine.

#strong[#emph[Quest.] How could his diuine nature be giuen vp? could it #footnote[How the person of Christ being Godman, was giuen for vs.] suffer? could it die?]

#emph[Answ.] 1. The Deitie simply considered in and by it selfe, could not die: but that person which was God, both could and did die. For the Sonne of God assuming an humane nature into the vnity of his diuine nature, and vniting them together #footnote[#emph[Symbol. Calced.]] #emph[without confusion, alteration, distraction, separation,] in one person, that which is done by one nature is done by the person, and in that respect the Scripture oft attributeth it to the other nature: as where it is said, #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 2. 8.] #emph[They crucified the Lord of glorie:] and #footnote[#emph[Act.] 20. 28.] #emph[God purchased the Churchwith his owne bloud.]

2. Though the diuine nature of Christ suffered not, yet did it support the humane nature, and adde dignity, worth and efficacie to the sufferings of that nature.

3. Christs diuine nature had proper and peculiar workes in the worke of redemption, as to sanctifie his humane nature, to take away our sinnes, to reconcile vs to God, and the like.

Thus then in three respects the whole person of Christ was giuen vnto vs.

1. In regard of the inseparable vnion of both natures.

2. In regard of the assistance of the Deity in those things which the humane nature of Christ did.

3. In regard of some proper actions appertaining to the Deity. #footnote[Doctr.]

In that the person of Christ God-Man was giuen vp, I gather that #footnote[1 #emph[Pet.] 1. 19. #emph[Vses.]]

#emph[The price of our Redemption is of infinite value.] Nor Christ, #footnote[#emph[Quanta se fiducia spes credentium consolatur considerans quantus quanta pro nondum credentibus passus sit? Aug. de Doctr. Chr. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 15.] nor God himselfe could giue a greater. Heauen and earth and all things in them are not of like worth. Well therefore might Saint #emph[Peter] call it #emph[pretious bloud:] and prefer it before siluer, gold, and all other things of price.

1. What place can be left for despaire in those that know and beleeue the worth of this ransome?

2. What can be held too deare for him, that notwithstanding the infinite excellency of his person gaue himselfe for vs? can goods, can friends, can children, can liberty, can life, can any thing else?

3. What iust cause haue we to #emph[giue vp our selues a liuing sacrifice,] #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 12. 1.] #emph[holy and acceptable] to him that gaue himselfe for vs?

4. How vngratefull, how vnworthy of Christ are they, that for his sake will not forsake their vnstable honours, fading wealth, vaine pleasures, garish attire, and such like trash?

=== §. 32. #emph[Of Christs seeking the good of the Church.]

The #emph[End] why Christ gaue himselfe was, #emph[for the Church: so] #footnote[Doctr.] #emph[as Christ in his death aimed at our good.] #footnote[2 #emph[Cor.] 5. 21.] He was made sinne #emph[for vs, that we might be made the righteousnesse of God in him:] #footnote[#emph[Gal.] 3. 13.] he was made a curse #emph[for vs, and hath redeemed vs from the curse of the Law:] #footnote[#emph[Gal.] 1. 4.] he gaue himselfe #emph[for our sinnes, that he might deliuer vs:] #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 10. 15.] he laid downe his life #emph[for the sheepe.]

This proues Christs giuing of himselfe to be a fruit of his loue: for #footnote[1\. #emph[Cor.] 13. 5. 1. #emph[Vse.]] #emph[Loue seeketh not her owne.]

Learne we hereby to apply all that Christ did to our selues. If #emph[for vs] he gaue himself, he, and #footnote[See §. 28. 2. #emph[Vse.]] all appertaining to him is ours.

Learne we also hereby how to manifest loue: namely by seeking, and procuring the good of others. #emph[Let no man seeke his] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 10. 24.] #emph[owne, but euery man anothers wealth.] If this were practised, would there be such oppressing, such vndermining, such deceiuing, such wronging of one another as there is? Too truly is the Apostles complaint verified in our daies, #emph[All seeke their] #footnote[#emph[Phil.] 2. 21.] #emph[owne.] But let that minde be in vs which was in Christ Iesus, and thus manifest our loue, as we desire to partake of this fruit of Christs loue.

From hence by iust consequence it followeth that #emph[Christ merited] #footnote[Christ merited not for himselfe.] #emph[not for himselfe.] Was there any need that Christ should come downe from heauen on earth, to purchase any thing for himselfe? When he was going out of the world, thus he praied, #emph[Now, O Father, glorifie thou me with the glorie which I had] #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 17. 5.] #emph[with thee before the world was.] Did Christ by any thing which he did on earth merit that glorie which he had before the world was? All the exaltation whereunto he was aduanced euen in his human nature, was due to the dignity of his person.

#strong[1. #emph[Obiect.] He endured the crosse, #emph[for the ioy which was set] #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 12. 2.] #emph[before him.]]

#emph[Answ.] He vsed that ioy which of right was due to him as an helpe to support him in the weaknesse of his humane nature, not as a recompence which he should deserue.

#strong[2. #emph[Obiect. He became obedient to the death of the Crosse,] #footnote[#emph[Phil.] 2. 9..] WHEREFORE #emph[God also hath highly exalted him.]]

#emph[Answ.] That particle #emph[(wherefore)] doth not declare the cause, but the order of his exaltation: nothing a consequence that followed after his death. After he had humbled himselfe so low, he was most highly aduanced.

#strong[3. #emph[Obiect.] Christ being man was bound to the Law: and therefore for himselfe he ought to fulfill it.]

#emph[Answ.] If he had beene meere man, that were true. But he vniting his humane nature vnto his diuine, and making of both one person, which person was God as well as man, he was bound to nothing further then it pleased him voluntarily to subiect himselfe vnto for our sakes.

2. If Christ were bound to the Law, of dutie he must haue fulfilled it: and if of dutie he was to fulfill it, how could he thereby merit so high a degree of honour as he is aduanced vnto?

This conceit of Christs meriting for himselfe, doth much extenuate the glorie of Christs grace and goodnesse in giuing himselfe.

=== §. 33. #emph[Of the particular ends, why Christ gaue himselfe, and of the condition of the Church before Christ tooke her.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[EPHES. 5. 26.]

    #emph[That he might sanctifie it, and cleanse it with the washing of water by the word.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

THe generall #emph[End] of Christs giuing himselfe being before intimated in this phrase #emph[(for vs)] is in this and the next verse particularly exemplified: and that in two branches.

One respecteth the estate of the Church in this world, #emph[v.] 26.

The other respecteth her estate in the world to come, #emph[v.] 27.

The latter of these two is the most principall.

The former is subordinate to the latter, an end for the accomplishing of the other end, for the Church is here made pure, that hereafter it may be made glorious.

In laying downe the former he noteth

- 1\. The #emph[end] whereat Christ aimed.
- 2\. The #emph[meanes,] wherby he effected that which he aimed at.

That #emph[end] is set forth in these words, #emph[that he might sanctifie it,] #footnote[See §. 39.] #emph[hauing cleansed it] (thus may they word for word be translated) so as that which for order of words is in the latter place, for order of matter is in the first place.

The word #emph[(cleansing)] pointeth out our #emph[instification.]

The word #emph[(sanctifying)] expresseth our #emph[sanctification.]

The meanes of effecting these, are two.

- 1\. #emph[Baptisme] comprised vnder this phrase, #emph[washing of water.]
- 2\. The #emph[word.]

The two branches of the former #emph[end,] namely #emph[Cleansing] and #emph[Sanctifying] doe in generall imply two things.

- 1\. The #emph[Condition] of the Church in it selfe.
- 2\. The #emph[Alteration] thereof by Christ.

The #emph[condition] is presupposed, which is, that she was impure, polluted, in the common estate of corrupt man. Things in themselues pure, are not cleansed, but things foule and impure: persons of themselues freed, and exempted from a common misery, need not anothers helpe to free and exempt them. Seeing then that the Church stood in need to be cleansed, and sanctified, surely

#emph[The Church in herselfe was, as the world, polluted.] Very liuely is #footnote[Doctr.] this set forth by the Prophet #emph[Ezekiel] vnder the similitude of #emph[a] #footnote[The Church in her selfe polluted.] #emph[wretched infant borne of a cursed parentage, whose nauell was not cut, who was not washed, salted, nor swadled, but cast out in the open field, polluted with bloud.] Oft doth the Apostle, setting forth #footnote[#emph[Ezek.] 16. 3. #emph[&c.]] the wretched estate of the world, note of the true members #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 2. 3.] of the Church, that #footnote[#emph[Tit.] 3. 3.] #emph[we our selues also were such.] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 11.]

The Church consisteth of none other then of such as came out of #emph[Adams] loines. Now as all the brood which commeth from vipers, adders, toads, spiders, and other like venomous dams, are infected with poison, so all the sonnes of #emph[Adam] are polluted with sinne. #emph[That which is borne of the flesh] (as is euery mothers childe, not the members of the Church excepted: for they haue fathers and mothers of their flesh) #emph[is flesh;] that is, polluted and corrupt. Therefore when we are taken into the Church, we are #emph[borne againe.] #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 3. 3, 5.]

This our former estate by nature is oft and seriously #footnote[Our naturall condition oft to be thought of.] to be thought of, and that in respect of

+ Christ.
+ Our selues.
+ Others.

1. In regard of Christ, the more to magnifie his loue. Our former estate, before he cast the wings of his mercy vpon vs, sheweth our vnworthinesse, our vilenesse, and wretchednesse, and in that respect it openeth our heart and mouth to thinke and say, #footnote[#emph[Psal.] 8. 1, 4.] #emph[O Lord our Lord, what is man that thou art mindfull of him, and the sonne of man that thou visitest him!] #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 14. 22.] #emph[Lord, how is it that thou wilt manifest thy selfe vnto vs, and not vnto the world!] The right knowledge of our former estate, and a due consideration thereof, maketh vs ascribe all the glory of our present dignity, and happinesse, to Christ that altered our estate, as Saint #emph[Paul,] #footnote[1 #emph[Tim.] 1. 12. #emph[&c.]] #emph[I thanke Christ Iesus our Lord who hath enabled mee, who was before a blasphemer, &c.] yea it maketh vs the more to prize and esteeme the present estate, as #footnote[2 #emph[Sam.] 7. 18, #emph[&c.]] #emph[Dauid.]

2. In regard of our selues this is to be thought of, to humble vs, and to keepe vs from insolent boasting in those priuiledges whereof through Christ we are made partakers. To this purpose doth the Apostle thus presse this point, #emph[Who maketh] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 4. 7.] #emph[thee to differ from another? and what hast thou that thou diddest not receiue? Now if thou diddest receiue it, why doest thou glory as if thou hadst not receiued it?] When a man is exalted from a meane, to a great place, and thereupon waxeth proud and insolent, we say, #emph[he hath forgotten from whence he came.] So as remembrance of our former condition is a meanes to preserue humility, and to suppresse insolencie.

3. In regard of others it is to be thought of, to moue vs the more to commiserate their wofull estate, who yet remaine as we once were; to conceiue hope that their estate may be altered as well as ours was; to pray and vse what meanes we can that it may be altered. To prouoke Christians to shew all meekenesse to them which were without, the Apostle renders this reason, #emph[for we our selues also in times past were foolish,] #footnote[#emph[Tit.] 3 3, #emph[&c.]] #emph[&c..] read how forcibly this is vrged, #emph[Rom.] 11. 18, 19, &c.
